title: Benchmarks
description: Throughput and latency benchmarks for QUIC and WebSockets transports, including performance under packet loss.
order: 5
---

# Benchmarks

These benchmarks measure the throughput and latency cost of the tunnel itself (encryption, framing, and multiplexing) and how each transport holds up under packet loss. The goal is to provide enough data to choose the right transport for the environment.

All results use `iperf3` pushing traffic through a full end-to-end tunnel with both nodes running and encryption active. Entry and exit run as QEMU microVMs on the same host. Results are from wallhackd v0.2.6 on Linux x86_64. To reproduce: `just bench benchmark` from the repo root.

## Loopback

No real network between the VMs. This isolates the cost of the tunnel itself (encryption, framing, and multiplexing) from network conditions.

### TCP Throughput

An `iperf3` client on the entry VM sends TCP traffic to an `iperf3` server bound to the exit VM's loopback address (`10.100.0.1`). Traffic flows through the entry TUN interface, the tunnel transport, and the exit adapter, which opens a separate TCP connection to the server. Each run is 10 seconds. Reverse flips the sender to the exit side; parallel opens N independent TCP streams.

| Transport | 1 stream | 2 streams | 4 streams | Reverse |
|-----------|----------|-----------|-----------|---------|
| QUIC | 1.4 Gbps | 1.3 Gbps | 1.1 Gbps | 218 Mbps |
| WebSocket | 1.3 Gbps | 1.4 Gbps | 1.5 Gbps | 565 Mbps |

WebSocket scales better with parallel streams and has higher reverse throughput.

### UDP Throughput

Same path as TCP, but `iperf3 -u -b 0` sends UDP datagrams with no bandwidth cap. The exit adapter forwards each datagram without flow control or congestion windowing on the inner connection.

| Transport | Throughput |
|-----------|------------|
| QUIC | 11.0 Gbps |
| WebSocket | 9.2 Gbps |

UDP throughput is an order of magnitude higher than TCP because there is no congestion window or retransmission overhead on the inner flow.

### Latency

`mean_rtt` from the entry VM kernel's `TCP_INFO` socket option (`tcpi_rtt`), reported by `iperf3 --json` over a 5-second run. This is the full round-trip as seen by the entry kernel's TCP stack: entry TUN, wallhack entry, tunnel transport, wallhack exit, exit adapter TCP connection to the `iperf3` server, and back.

| Transport | RTT |
|-----------|-----|
| QUIC | 0.52 ms |
| WebSocket | 0.81 ms |

End-to-end RTT through the full tunnel stays sub-millisecond on loopback for both transports.

## Synthetic Packet Loss

Same loopback topology with [netem](https://wiki.linuxfoundation.org/networking/netem) (`tc qdisc add dev eth0 root netem`) applied on the entry VM's ethernet interface. Netem acts on egress, so loss and delay affect the entry-to-exit direction only. 4 parallel TCP streams (`iperf3 -P 4`) over a 10-second run.

| Transport | 0.5% loss / 5ms delay | 2% loss / 25ms delay |
|-----------|----------------------|----------------------|
| QUIC | 1.2 Gbps | 1.3 Gbps |
| WebSocket | 1.5 Gbps | 1.5 Gbps |

On a high-bandwidth loopback link, both transports absorb modest loss without measurable throughput degradation. Retransmissions complete almost immediately because the underlying link has no bandwidth constraint.
